docs/decisions.md:1158:| OD43 | <a id="od43"></a>How a type position resolves a name (added 2026-10-29, from the spec gap bug 229's ruling found). Options: (a) the current compiler's rules: type positions search the type namespace only, a user `enum Option` is always the builtin, and a parent sees its child's newtypes; (b) a user declaration shadows a builtin of the same name, with a warning; (c) a user type may not take a builtin type's name (refused). Recommendation: (c), which removes the shadowing question entirely; Austral reserves its builtin type names the same way Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (c) refused, for builtin-name collisions only, with the complete reserved set published (also for aliases and import renames); this answer would not retire OD43, whose type-versus-value lookup and child-module questions stay open in it. A user `struct Copy HeapArray` is treated as linear today | the current compiler follows (a) | MOD-1; bug 229's record | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse builtin-name collisions, publish reserved set. Completed 2026-11-02 by the owner: one namespace per module, and a child module's declarations only through `child::T` or an import ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-namespace)) |
docs/decisions.md:1162:| OD47 | <a id="od47"></a>Loops: `while` and a counted range loop (added 2026-10-29) | the current compiler has `while` and the C-style `for (init; cond; step)`, `for (cond)` and `for { }`; bug 331 | exit 2q; R-0499; V1a's loops and exits | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: (1) the C-style `for` is removed in every form; (2) loops are `while cond { ... }` and `for i from a until b { ... }`: half-open (`a`, ..., `b - 1`, empty when `a >= b`), the index immutable in the body, `a` and `b` evaluated once before the first iteration, the index of the bounds' integer type (both bounds of one type), `break` and `continue` as usual (`continue` advances to the next index), `from` and `until` reserved (whether reserved or contextual was reopened by the reviews of that day: OD50), LL(1) as `for` IDENT `from` expr `until` expr block with the restricted head expression for `b` (OD16); (3) in Core the counted loop desugars to the one `while` form, carrying its termination measure `b - i`. Now STMT-6@3, STMT-11@2 and STMT-12 (pending, bug 331) |
docs/decisions.md:1165:| OD50 | <a id="od50"></a>Reserved or contextual `from` and `until` (added 2026-10-29, from the reviews of OD47). Options: (a) reserved words everywhere, as OD47 first said: `let from: Int` is refused and the lexer has two more keywords; (b) contextual keywords, meaningful only between `for IDENT` and the block, so existing names stay legal. Recommendation: (a), one lexical rule and no context-dependent tokens, at the cost of two names; Austral reserves its loop words Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (a) reserved everywhere | the current compiler has neither word (bug 331) | bug 331's repair; SYN-1 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Reserved everywhere |
docs/decisions.md:1179:| OD64 | <a id="od64"></a>Raw pointers derived from a borrow (added 2026-10-30, item G19 of the owner picks of that day). Question: What a raw pointer derived from a borrow may do: whether it is valid only while the borrow lives, may escape the borrow block, may be passed to C, and how extern parameters say so. Options: (a) valid only while the borrow lives and never escaping it; (b) the operational loan rules of REF-14 only. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a): no returning, no escaping storage (a local `let` inside the block stays allowed), no converting to an integer and no capturing in an outer `defer`; borrow-derived pointers go only to call-only `extern` parameters; C uses it only during a call (memory C keeps must come from an allocation with its own owner); `extern` parameters say call-only (the default) or kept; field pointers come from the same borrow of the whole owner; deferred calls in the block run before the borrow ends; provenance survives copies, offsets, field pointers, helpers and indirect calls; a shared borrow never yields write access; `ptr::expose` is refused on such a pointer; the annotation's syntax gets an LL(1) check. Austral: not checked | no pass assumes `&mut` exclusivity (OD15); REF-14 bounds aliases by their loan | REF-14, TRUST-1, TRUST-3, FFI-3, OWN-12 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Valid only inside the borrow; call-only extern params |
docs/decisions.md:1188:| OD73 | <a id="od73"></a>Reserved words used as identifiers (added 2026-10-30, item P5 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether `cap` and `type`, which the lexer reserves, are refused as identifiers where the parser lets them through. Options: (a) refused, reserved words stay reserved; (b) the grammar lets them through there. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a) refuse. Austral: keywords are reserved | `expectIdent` accepts `cap` (and `type`) as a variant name | SYN-1 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse |
docs/decisions.md:1196:| OD81 | <a id="od81"></a>`ghost let` in the grammar (added 2026-10-30, item C4 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether the grammar gains the proof-only binding `ghost let` (with `ghost` a contextual keyword). Options: (a) add it to the grammar; (b) remove it from the parser, so the language loses the form; (c) keep the recorded divergence for now. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): individual review required; no pick recorded. Austral: not applicable | the parser accepts it | CON-4, SYN-1 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Add, with `ghost` reserved |
docs/decisions.md:1205:| OD90 | <a id="od90"></a>`struct Copy <T>` and contextual keywords (added 2026-10-30, item Copy of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether `Copy` stays a contextual keyword, with the LL(1) checkers taught to model it, or becomes a reserved word (the other unreserved words, `self`, `repr`, `packed` and `align`, stay as they are unless decided separately). Options: (a) reserve `Copy` as a keyword, refusing it as an identifier; (b) keep it contextual, the LL(1) checkers taught to model a quoted unreserved terminal as also IDENT, each conflict found listed for the owner (letters as in the divergence report). Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a) reserve `Copy`: one lexical rule, consistent with OD50 (reserved `from` and `until`) and OD43 (builtin names refused as user names). Austral: its keywords are reserved | contextual; the `struct Copy <T>` conflict is invisible to the checkers' token model | SYN-1, OWN-D1 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Reserve `Copy` |
docs/decisions.md:1246:| OD131 | <a id="od131"></a>`union` declarations (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the compiler documents and the grammar comments). Question: Whether the language has `union` declarations, and if so what reading a field other than the one last written means. Bears on SYN-1, TYPE-7, FFI-7 and FFI-8. The current compiler: `union U { a: Int, b: Uint }` parses (`Concrete/Frontend/Parser.lean`, as a struct whose fields share storage) and compiles; a value built as `U { a: 42 }` is linear, and `union` is neither a keyword nor in the grammar. No document specifies it. Options: (a) remove it; (b) keep it, for the foreign boundary only, with its layout and read rules specified. Austral: no unions. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/compiler, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-131 | see OPEN-131 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Remove |
docs/decisions.md:1248:| OD133 | <a id="od133"></a>User functions named `print`, `println` or `append` (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the compiler documents and the grammar comments). Question: Whether a program may declare a function named `print`, `println` or `append`, which then replaces the builtin of that name in calls the program writes. Bears on MOD-3, MOD-8 and TYPE-10. The current compiler: accepted; with `fn print(x: Int) -> Int`, `print(42)` calls it (exit 42 on both consumers), because Elab desugars the variadic builtins only when no function of that name is visible, and MOD-3's reserved names do not include them. MOD-8 already binds the calls the compiler itself generates by identity. Options: (a) reserve the three names (MOD-3); (b) allow the replacement, as the current compiler does. Austral: not applicable. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/compiler, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-133 | see OPEN-133 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Reserve the three names |
docs/decisions.md:1250:| OD135 | <a id="od135"></a>A method's `Type_method` name in its module's namespace (added 2026-10-30, raised while reviewing the absorption of docs/compiler and docs/platform). Question: Whether an impl method `m` of a type `T` also occupies the name `T_m` in its module, so that a free function of that name is refused. Bears on MOD-3 and MOD-8. The current compiler: refused: `fn P_get(a: Int) -> Int` beside `impl P { fn get(&self) -> Int }` is E0299 under MOD-3@2 ("a function named 'P_get' is also declared in this module as an impl method"), on both consumers, because the method is entered under its mangled name. MOD-3's reserved names are the `__` prefix, `user_main` and the builtins, which do not include such names. Options: (a) keep it, listing `T_m` among the names MOD-3 reserves; (b) stop entering methods under `T_m`, so the free function is accepted. Austral: not applicable (no methods). Pick: none given (raised by the review of the absorption of docs/compiler, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-135 | see OPEN-135 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Stop entering methods under `T_m` |
docs/decisions.md:1257:| OD142 | <a id="od142"></a>`X.con` beside `X/mod.con` (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Which file `mod X;` loads when both `X.con` and `X/mod.con` exist. Bears on MOD-4. The current compiler: `X.con` is loaded and `X/mod.con` is ignored without a diagnostic (with `X.con` returning 7 and `X/mod.con` 9, the program exits 7 on both consumers). MOD-4 says one module is loaded from either file. Options: (a) refuse the pair; (b) `X.con` takes precedence, as the current compiler does; (c) `X/mod.con` takes precedence. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-142 | see OPEN-142 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): One module layout: `X.con`, children in directory `X/`; no `mod.con` |
docs/decisions.md:1268:| OD153 | <a id="od153"></a>Program functions named like a builtin (added 2026-10-31, raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2). Question: Whether a program may declare a function whose name is a builtin's, other than `print`, `println` and `append` (OPEN-133) and the names MOD-3 reserves, and which function the program's calls of that name reach. Bears on MOD-3, MOD-1, MOD-8 and OPEN-133. The current compiler: a program function `string_length` is called by the program's calls on both consumers, and so are program functions named `string_len` and `String_len` (`string_len` returns 7 and `String_len` returns 9; a program that calls both exits 79 on both consumers), while `destroy`, `discard`, `abort`, `alloc`, `free`, `alloc_array`, `free_array` and `realloc_array` are reserved (E0578). The calls the compiler itself generates are bound by identity (MOD-8), so only the program's own calls change. Options: (a) reserve every remaining builtin's name, aliases included, and MOD-3 lists them (`print`, `println` and `append` stay with OPEN-133); (b) allow the declaration, and the program's own calls reach it, as the current compiler does, and MOD-3 says so; (c) allow the declaration, and a program reaches the builtin by a qualified name. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-153 | see OPEN-153 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Refuse program functions named like builtins |
docs/decisions.md:1361:    the trusted base, with its identity preserved through interfaces, specialization and linking.
docs/decisions.md:1369:13. **Capability names are reserved and differ from resource type names**: `with(Fs)` against a
docs/decisions.md:1370:    `File` value. The reserved set is `Fs`, `Network`, `Env`, `Time`, `Alloc`, `Device`, `Console`, `Process` and
docs/decisions.md:1372:    `StdErr` grants, added by the [owner revision, 2026-11-05](#decided-2026-11-05-revisions), row Q7.11; there is no `Net` spelling and no alias). They are reserved everywhere,
docs/decisions.md:1462:- **Deferred arguments.** A by-value argument of a deferred call is reserved and read when the call runs
