117:The token forms (SYN-6), the statement `match` (SYN-8), the value `if` (SYN-9) and the separators
278:<a id="SYN-2"></a>
279:### SYN-2. Operators group by a fixed precedence
281:**SYN-2@1.** Binary operators group left to right at the precedence levels the grammar lists before `expr`, from `||` (lowest) through `&&`, equality, comparison, `|`, `^`, `&`, shifts, `+ -` and `* / %` to `as`, and postfix operations bind tighter than prefix ones, which bind tighter than `as`.
324:<a id="SYN-3"></a>
325:### SYN-3. Compiler spellings are reserved
327:**SYN-3@1.** A `let` does not bind a name that begins with `__destr_`, the prefix of the bindings the compiler introduces when it lowers a struct destructuring.
426:### SYN-5. Source text and string literals are UTF-8
428:**SYN-5@3.** A source file is UTF-8 text, a string literal denotes the UTF-8 encoding of its characters, and its only escapes are `\n`, `\t`, `\r`, `\\`, `\"` and `\0`, so a file that is not valid UTF-8 and any other `\` sequence in a literal are not accepted, and every `String<A>` reachable by safe code holds valid UTF-8.
440:knowing the editor or the platform. Unknown escapes were once read as the character itself; the
441:lexer now refuses them, with E0001 tagged SYN-1 (`unknown string escape '\q'`). A file that is not
442:valid UTF-8 is refused when it is read, before lexing, but today by an uncaught exception
443:(`Tried to read file ... containing non UTF-8 data`) rather than a diagnostic. There is no `\xHH`
444:or `\u{...}` escape. OD127 guarantees valid UTF-8 in every `String<A>` reachable by safe code; safe constructors validate, and unchecked construction requires a trusted validity premise.
453:<a id="SYN-6"></a>
454:### SYN-6. Tokens
456:**SYN-6@3.** A token is an identifier (a letter or `_`, then letters, digits and `_`), an integer literal in decimal or with a `0x`, `0b` or `0o` prefix, a float literal written as digits, `.` and digits, a string literal, a char literal (one ASCII character, or one of the escapes `\n`, `\t`, `\r`, `\0`, `\\` and `\'`, between single quotes), a label (`'` then an identifier), a keyword, or an operator or punctuation mark, and `//` begins a comment that ends at the end of its line.
493:<a id="SYN-7"></a>
494:### SYN-7. `>>` closes two type-argument lists
496:**SYN-7@1.** Where a `>` closes a type-argument or type-parameter list, a `>>` token closes two nested lists, read as two `>`.
605:**SYN-10@1.** `.` selects a field or calls a method, `::` qualifies a member of a module, a variant of an enum or an associated function of a type and introduces explicit type arguments, and an import path separates its modules with `.`.
663:- SYN-6@2 (spec audits 6 and 10, 2026-10-30) adds the char-literal escapes to the sentence; the compiler accepted them before.
667:- SYN-6@3 (2026-11-03) brings the chapter into line with the recorded owner decisions; SYN-6@2 is superseded by version.
- Uses: none
- Codes: E0283 (check)
- Status: enforced
- Enforced by: Concrete.checkStmt
- Semantics: none
- Obligation: none
- Evidence: tested

**Rationale.** The post-specialization reference check (REF-6) exempts the compiler's
destructuring temporaries by their prefix, so a source binding with that spelling would inherit
the exemption. Function names the compiler reserves (`__` names, `user_main` and the builtins)
are refused by MOD-3.

```con reject name=compiler-spelling code=E0283 stage=check pair=ordinary-spelling
fn example_value() -> i64 {
    let __destr_x: i64 = 42;
    return __destr_x;
}
fn main() -> u8 {
    if example_value() == 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}
```

```con accept name=ordinary-spelling exit=42
fn example_value() -> i64 {
    let destr_x: i64 = 42;
    return destr_x;
}
fn main() -> u8 {
    if example_value() == 42 {
        return 42;
    }
    return 1;
}
```

<a id="SYN-4"></a>
### SYN-4. A struct literal in a condition head is parenthesized

**SYN-4@2.** A struct literal in the head of an `if`, `while` or `match` is refused unless enclosed in parentheses or brackets, including inside call arguments, and a `{` outside those delimiters ends the head and opens its block regardless of the preceding name.

- Kind: refusal
- Uses: none
- Codes: E0001 (parse)
- Status: pending R-0555
- Semantics: none
- Obligation: none
- Evidence: tested

**Rationale.** OD16 and OD34 fix head disambiguation without consulting name resolution; the legacy parser remains pending (bug 318).

```con reject name=bare-struct-literal-in-head code=E0001 stage=parse pair=parenthesized-struct-literal-in-head pending=bug-318 now=accept:42
struct Copy P { v: i64 }

fn main() -> u8 {
    if P { v: 1 }.v == 1 {
        return 42;
    }
    return 0;
}
```

```con accept name=parenthesized-struct-literal-in-head exit=42
struct Copy P { v: i64 }
fn one(p: P) -> bool { return p.v == 1; }
fn main() -> u8 { if one(P { v: 1 }) { return 42; } return 0; }
```

```con accept name=head-constant-unparenthesized exit=42 pending=bug-318 now=reject:E0001:parse
const LIMIT: i64 = 3;

fn example_value() -> i64 {
    let mut n: i64 = 0;
    while n < LIMIT {
        n = n + 1;
    }
    return n + 39;
}

fn main() -> u8 {

    if example_value() == 42 {

        return 42;

    }

    return 1;

}
```

<a id="SYN-5"></a>
### SYN-5. Source text and string literals are UTF-8

**SYN-5@3.** A source file is UTF-8 text, a string literal denotes the UTF-8 encoding of its characters, and its only escapes are `\n`, `\t`, `\r`, `\\`, `\"` and `\0`, so a file that is not valid UTF-8 and any other `\` sequence in a literal are not accepted, and every `String<A>` reachable by safe code holds valid UTF-8.

- Kind: guarantee
- Uses: none
- Codes: none
- Status: pending R-0555
- Enforced by: Concrete.lexStringLoop
- Semantics: none
- Obligation: none
- Evidence: tested

**Rationale.** One encoding for source and literals means a literal's bytes are known without
knowing the editor or the platform. Unknown escapes were once read as the character itself; the
lexer now refuses them, with E0001 tagged SYN-1 (`unknown string escape '\q'`). A file that is not
valid UTF-8 is refused when it is read, before lexing, but today by an uncaught exception
(`Tried to read file ... containing non UTF-8 data`) rather than a diagnostic. There is no `\xHH`
or `\u{...}` escape. OD127 guarantees valid UTF-8 in every `String<A>` reachable by safe code; safe constructors validate, and unchecked construction requires a trusted validity premise.






Static-text type and borrowing are [undecided: see the target Core proposal](decisions.md#core-static-text); the legacy literal-library examples are retired.

<a id="SYN-6"></a>
### SYN-6. Tokens

**SYN-6@3.** A token is an identifier (a letter or `_`, then letters, digits and `_`), an integer literal in decimal or with a `0x`, `0b` or `0o` prefix, a float literal written as digits, `.` and digits, a string literal, a char literal (one ASCII character, or one of the escapes `\n`, `\t`, `\r`, `\0`, `\\` and `\'`, between single quotes), a label (`'` then an identifier), a keyword, or an operator or punctuation mark, and `//` begins a comment that ends at the end of its line.

- Kind: guarantee
- Uses: none
- Codes: none
- Status: pending R-0555
- Enforced by: Concrete.tokenize
- Semantics: none
- Obligation: none
- Evidence: tested

**Rationale.** OD137 limits character literals to ASCII; char values remain bytes (TYPE-20).

```con accept name=token-forms exit=42
// a line comment
fn example_value() -> i64 {
    let a: i64 = 0o17;
    let b: i64 = 0x1F;
    let c: i64 = 0b101;
    let q: char = '\'';
96:/-- Lex an identifier or keyword. -/
316:/-- Lex a string literal (after opening quote). Only the escapes listed here
332:      (s.advance, .lexError s!"unknown string escape '\\{c}' (valid escapes: \\n \\t \\r \\0 \\\\ \\\")")
374:      (s.advance, .lexError s!"unknown character escape '\\{c}' (valid escapes: \\n \\t \\r \\0 \\\\ \\')")
import Concrete.Frontend.Token

namespace Concrete

structure LexerState where
  source : Array Char
  pos : Nat
  line : Nat
  col : Nat
  deriving Repr, Inhabited

def LexerState.init (source : String) : LexerState :=
  { source := source.toList.toArray, pos := 0, line := 1, col := 1 }

def LexerState.peek (s : LexerState) : Option Char :=
  if h : s.pos < s.source.size then some s.source[s.pos]
  else none

def LexerState.peekAt (s : LexerState) (offset : Nat) : Option Char :=
  let idx := s.pos + offset
  if h : idx < s.source.size then some s.source[idx]
  else none

def LexerState.advance (s : LexerState) : LexerState :=
  match s.peek with
  | some '\n' => { s with pos := s.pos + 1, line := s.line + 1, col := 1 }
  | some _ => { s with pos := s.pos + 1, col := s.col + 1 }
  | none => s

def LexerState.span (s : LexerState) : Span :=
  { line := s.line, col := s.col }

def LexerState.atEnd (s : LexerState) : Bool :=
  s.pos ≥ s.source.size

def lookupKeyword : String → Option TokenKind
  | "fn" => some .fn
  | "let" => some .«let»
  | "mut" => some .mut
  | "if" => some .if_
  | "else" => some .else_
  | "while" => some .while_
  | "for" => some .for_
  | "return" => some .return_
  | "assert" => some .assert_
  | "assume" => some .assume_
  | "true" => some .true_
  | "false" => some .false_
  | "mod" => some .«mod»
  | "struct" => some .struct_
  | "enum" => some .enum_
  | "match" => some .match_
  | "pub" => some .pub_
  | "import" => some .import_
  | "as" => some .as_
  | "impl" => some .impl_
  | "trait" => some .trait_
  | "const" => some .const_
  | "type" => some .type_
  | "extern" => some .extern_
  | "with" => some .with_
  | "cap" => some .cap_
  | "break" => some .break_
  | "continue" => some .continue_
  | "defer" => some .defer_
  | "borrow" => some .borrow_
  | "in" => some .in_
  | "newtype" => some .newtype_
  | "trusted" => some .trusted_
  | _ => none

private def isIdentStart (c : Char) : Bool :=
  c.isAlpha || c == '_'

private def isIdentCont (c : Char) : Bool :=
  c.isAlphanum || c == '_'

/-- Skip whitespace and line comments. -/
partial def skipWhitespace (s : LexerState) : LexerState :=
  match s.peek with
  | some ' ' | some '\t' | some '\n' | some '\r' =>
    skipWhitespace s.advance
  | some '/' =>
    let s2 := s.advance
    match s2.peek with
    | some '/' => skipLineComment s2.advance
    | _ => s
  | _ => s
where
  skipLineComment (s : LexerState) : LexerState :=
    match s.peek with
    | some '\n' => skipWhitespace s.advance
    | some _ => skipLineComment s.advance
    | none => s

/-- Lex an identifier or keyword. -/
partial def lexIdentLoop (s : LexerState) (acc : String) : LexerState × TokenKind :=
  match s.peek with
  | some c =>
    if isIdentCont c then
      lexIdentLoop s.advance (acc.push c)
    else
      (s, (lookupKeyword acc).getD (.ident acc))
  | none =>
    (s, (lookupKeyword acc).getD (.ident acc))

/-- Lex hex digits after 0x/0X prefix. -/
partial def lexHexLoop (s : LexerState) (acc : Nat) : LexerState × TokenKind :=
  match s.peek with
  | some c =>
    if c.isDigit then
      lexHexLoop s.advance (acc * 16 + (c.toNat - '0'.toNat))
    else if c >= 'a' && c <= 'f' then
      lexHexLoop s.advance (acc * 16 + (c.toNat - 'a'.toNat + 10))
    else if c >= 'A' && c <= 'F' then
      lexHexLoop s.advance (acc * 16 + (c.toNat - 'A'.toNat + 10))
    else
      (s, .intLit acc)
  | none => (s, .intLit acc)

/-- Lex binary digits after 0b/0B prefix. -/
partial def lexBinLoop (s : LexerState) (acc : Nat) : LexerState × TokenKind :=
  match s.peek with
  | some c =>
    if c == '0' || c == '1' then
      lexBinLoop s.advance (acc * 2 + (c.toNat - '0'.toNat))
    else
      (s, .intLit acc)
  | none => (s, .intLit acc)

/-- Lex octal digits after 0o/0O prefix. -/
partial def lexOctLoop (s : LexerState) (acc : Nat) : LexerState × TokenKind :=
  match s.peek with
  | some c =>
    if c >= '0' && c <= '7' then
      lexOctLoop s.advance (acc * 8 + (c.toNat - '0'.toNat))
    else
      (s, .intLit acc)
  | none => (s, .intLit acc)

/-- Correctly-rounded decimal → binary64: the nearest double to
    `mantissa × 10^(-exp10)`, ties to even, by exact big-`Nat` arithmetic.
    `Float.ofScientific` keeps only ~11 guard bits after dividing out `5^exp10`
    and lands one ulp off on rare literals (corpus: `16.3633343`,
    `932183.9385014`); this keeps all sticky information and never mis-rounds.
    Overflows to +Inf like strtod; underflows gradually through subnormals to +0. -/
def floatOfDecimalMantissa (mantissa : Nat) (exp10 : Nat) : Float :=
  if mantissa == 0 then 0.0
  else
    -- v = mantissa / (den · 2^exp10), exactly
    let den := 5 ^ exp10
    -- e = ⌊log₂ v⌋; the estimate e0 satisfies e ∈ {e0, e0+1}.
    -- v ≥ 2^(e0+1) ⟺ mantissa/den ≥ 2^(e0+1+exp10) (the 2^exp10 is inside gePow2's t)
    let e0 := (mantissa.log2 : Int) - ((den.log2 : Int) + 1) - (exp10 : Int)
    let e := if gePow2 mantissa den (e0 + 1 + (exp10 : Int)) then e0 + 1 else e0
    if e ≥ -1022 then
      -- normal: significand = round(v / 2^(e-52))
      let sig := roundScaled mantissa den ((52 : Int) - e - (exp10 : Int))
      let (sig, e) := if sig ≥ 2^53 then (sig / 2, e + 1) else (sig, e)
      if e > 1023 then
        Float.ofBits 0x7FF0000000000000
      else
        Float.ofBits (((e + 1023).toNat.toUInt64) <<< 52 ||| (sig - 2^52).toUInt64)
    else
      -- subnormal: significand = round(v / 2^(-1074))
      let sig := roundScaled mantissa den ((1074 : Int) - (exp10 : Int))
      if sig == 0 then 0.0
      else if sig ≥ 2^52 then Float.ofBits ((1 : UInt64) <<< 52)  -- rounded up to min normal
      else Float.ofBits sig.toUInt64
where
  /-- `num/den ≥ 2^t`, by exact big-`Nat` comparison (num, den > 0). -/
  gePow2 (num den : Nat) (t : Int) : Bool :=
    if t ≥ 0 then num ≥ den <<< t.toNat
    else num <<< (-t).toNat ≥ den
  /-- Exact round-to-nearest (ties to even) of `num · 2^shift / den`
14:### ARITH-1. Floating point is IEEE-754 with round-to-nearest-even
16:**ARITH-1@1.** `f32` and `f64` are IEEE-754 binary32 and binary64, every operation rounds to nearest with ties to even, and no operations are contracted (no fused multiply-add unless written).
