553:### ARITH-11. The named float conversions round as stated
555:**ARITH-11@2.** A named conversion from an integer to a float, or from `f64` to `f32`, rounds to the nearest representable value with ties to even, and each float-to-integer rounding operation (`trunc`, `round`, `floor`, `ceil`) rounds as named and returns the resulting integer when representable, or an error as `try_convert` for NaN, infinities and results outside the target integer range.
565:**Rationale.** These are the conversions ARITH-5 sends a rounding `as` to (`i32` to `f32`, `i64` to
566:`f64`, `f64` to `f32`, every float to an integer). Today `as` rounds an integer to a float natively
567:and is the identity in `--interp` (bug 128), and a float-to-integer `as` truncates toward zero and
568:aborts on NaN, an infinity or a value out of range. OD107 instead requires an error as `try_convert`; OD99 fixes the builtin spellings `trunc`, `round`, `floor`, `ceil`. Implementation remains pending R-0500.
