1221:| OD106 | <a id="od106"></a>The result type of a checked conversion (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a checked conversion (`try_convert`) reports a value it cannot preserve as `Option::None` or as a `Result` error carrying the reason. Bears on ARITH-5, ARITH-10. The current compiler: no checked conversion exists (E0101, R-0500). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 wrote `try_convert::<T>(x) -> Option<T>` "(or a `Result` with a reason)" and left the choice to the API's owner. Options: (a) `Option<T>`; (b) a `Result` whose error names the reason (out of range, not integral, NaN, infinite, not representable). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | no checked conversion exists (E0101, R-0500) | ARITH-5, ARITH-10 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `Result<T, OutOfRange>` (failure, not absence) |
1222:| OD107 | <a id="od107"></a>What a float-to-integer rounding gives for NaN, infinities and out-of-range values (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether each float-to-integer rounding operation (`trunc`, `round`, `floor`, `ceil`) gives a value or an error for NaN, an infinity or a result outside the target type's range, and which value. Bears on ARITH-1, ARITH-11. The current compiler: the rounding operations do not exist (R-0500); a float-to-integer `as` truncates toward zero and aborts on NaN, an infinity or a value out of range, natively and in `--interp`. The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 required a stated result for every input, "either as a value or as an error". Options: (a) an error, as `try_convert` reports; (b) a value: the nearest value of the type for an out-of-range input or an infinity, and 0 for NaN; (c) a trap, as `as` does today. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | the rounding operations do not exist; a float-to-integer `as` truncates toward zero and aborts on NaN, an infinity or a value out of range | ARITH-1, ARITH-11 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): An error, as `try_convert` |
1229:| OD114 | <a id="od114"></a>How integer division rounds (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: How an integer division that does not abort rounds its quotient, and which sign a nonzero remainder takes. Bears on ARITH-3. The current compiler: the quotient is truncated toward zero and a nonzero remainder has the sign of the dividend, as C99 and LLVM's `sdiv` and `srem` give (`-7 / 2` is `-3`, `-7 % 2` is `-1`, `7 % -2` is `1`), in the binary, the interpreter and the constant folder (`IntArith.evalIntBinOp`). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) states when division aborts (§5) but not how it rounds. Options: (a) truncation toward zero, as in the current compiler; (b) floored division, the remainder taking the divisor's sign; (c) Euclidean division, the remainder never negative. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | the quotient is truncated toward zero and a remainder has the dividend's sign (C99) | ARITH-3 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Truncation toward zero |
1236:| OD121 | <a id="od121"></a>Projections from a base that is not a traceable place (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether a field access, index or `*` whose base is not a traceable place (an `if` or `match` value, `**r`, a reference read out of storage) is refused, or accepted once the checker can trace it. Bears on REF-5, MEM-4, MEM-8, TYPE-7. The current compiler: refused (E0238, `untraceablePlace`), whether the projection reads a Copy value or forms a reference: `(if c { a } else { b }).f`, `(**r).f`, `*w.r`. No entry states the refusal: REF-5 forbids a branch value or a stored reference as a reference's root, and MEM-4, MEM-8 and TYPE-7 do not restrict a projection's base. [KNOWN_HOLES.md](../legacy/docs/verification/KNOWN_HOLES.md) H28 calls it a deliberate restriction until R-0499's checker on Core. Options: (a) state the restriction as a rule (a projection's base is a place: a binding, its fields and elements, or `*` of a named reference); (b) accept such projections of owned values and Copy reads once the checker traces them, refusing only what REF-5 refuses. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-121 | see OPEN-121 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): State the restriction as a rule |
1491:- <a id="OPEN-52"></a>**OPEN-52** (OD52, `if let`, `while let` and `let ... else`): See [OD52](#od52).
1515:- <a id="OPEN-107"></a>**OPEN-107** (OD107, What a float-to-integer rounding gives for NaN, infinities and out-of-range values): See [OD107](#od107).
1519:- <a id="OPEN-114"></a>**OPEN-114** (OD114, How integer division rounds): See [OD114](#od114).
| OD113 | <a id="od113"></a>The ownership exceptions of trusted code (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether trusted code keeps its two exceptions to the ownership rules, destroying a value through a reference and releasing with `vec_free` a vector whose elements are not Copy. Bears on TRUST-5, TRUST-12, OWN-5, OWN-8. The current compiler: both are accepted in trusted code only (elsewhere E0290 under OWN-8 and E0287 under OWN-5): std's collections destroy an element through `(p as &T)` in drop glue after its owner was consumed, and a trusted function may free a buffer it has emptied. The archived [MEMORY_SEMANTICS.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/MEMORY_SEMANTICS.md) §11 said that `trusted` does not relax linearity, and TRUST-5's rationale says the same. Options: (a) keep both, each a named trusted operation with its premise (the owner was consumed; the vector is empty); (b) remove both, drop glue moving the element out of raw memory instead (TRUST-7); (c) keep only destruction through a reference; (d) keep only `vec_free` of an emptied vector. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | both accepted in trusted code only (E0290, E0287 elsewhere) | TRUST-5, TRUST-12, OWN-5, OWN-8 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Remove both |
| OD114 | <a id="od114"></a>How integer division rounds (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: How an integer division that does not abort rounds its quotient, and which sign a nonzero remainder takes. Bears on ARITH-3. The current compiler: the quotient is truncated toward zero and a nonzero remainder has the sign of the dividend, as C99 and LLVM's `sdiv` and `srem` give (`-7 / 2` is `-3`, `-7 % 2` is `-1`, `7 % -2` is `1`), in the binary, the interpreter and the constant folder (`IntArith.evalIntBinOp`). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) states when division aborts (§5) but not how it rounds. Options: (a) truncation toward zero, as in the current compiler; (b) floored division, the remainder taking the divisor's sign; (c) Euclidean division, the remainder never negative. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | the quotient is truncated toward zero and a remainder has the dividend's sign (C99) | ARITH-3 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Truncation toward zero |
| OD115 | <a id="od115"></a>The body and totality of a specification function (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether a `spec fn` may be declared without a body, and whether its body must be shown total. Bears on CON-3, CON-5, CON-1. The current compiler: both `spec fn big(x: Int) -> bool;` and `spec fn big(x: Int) -> bool = x > 10;` are accepted, and so is a recursive body that never terminates (`spec fn loopy(x: Int) -> bool = loopy(x + 1);`), with no check. [CONTRACTS_GUIDE.md](../legacy/docs/verification/CONTRACTS_GUIDE.md) calls a `spec fn` "an erased mathematical specification, no body"; contracts.md's examples (CON-3) give it a body; [CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md](../legacy/docs/verification/CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md) (Soundness Bridge) requires it to be total. Options: (a) a body is required and must be total (structural recursion or a checked measure); (b) a body is optional, a body-less one an uninterpreted function a proof must constrain, and a body must be total; (c) as in the current compiler, no totality check. Austral: not applicable. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-115 | see OPEN-115 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Body required and total |
| OD99 | <a id="od99"></a>The names of the named conversions (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: The spellings of the named conversions of ARITH-5, ARITH-10 and ARITH-11 (`try_convert`, `truncate`, `reinterpret`, the integer-to-float and `f64`-to-`f32` conversions, and the float roundings `trunc`, `round`, `floor`, `ceil`). Bears on ARITH-5, ARITH-10, ARITH-11. The current compiler: none of them exists (E0101). ARITH-5's sentence names `truncate` and `reinterpret`, while the archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 calls its spellings indicative, to be fixed by the API's owner; it names the integer-to-float and `f64`-to-`f32` conversions by their rounding only. Options: (a) the spellings above, as builtins; (b) other names, or std functions rather than builtins. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-99 | see OPEN-99 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): These spellings, as builtins |
| OD100 | <a id="od100"></a>Comparisons on a type parameter (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether a comparison on a value of a `Copy`-bounded type parameter is accepted and checked again at each instantiation, or refused in the generic body. Bears on TYPE-6, TYPE-9, PASS-1, OPEN-42. The current compiler: `a < b` with `a, b: T` and `T: Copy` is accepted, and an instantiation at a type with no comparison is refused after monomorphization (E0229, `post-mono`); without the `Copy` bound it is refused in the body (E0229, `check`). TYPE-9's rationale says a generic body is checked once, from its bounds, which a per-instantiation check departs from. Options: (a) keep the current compiler's per-instantiation check; (b) refuse a comparison on a type parameter until a bound states it (a comparison trait); (c) refuse it. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md §8a (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-100 | see OPEN-100 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse until a bound states it |
| OD101 | <a id="od101"></a>What a trap writes (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether a run-time trap writes a message naming the operation, its operands and its source location before the process ends, and in what form. Bears on STMT-2, STMT-7, ARITH-3. The current compiler: the binary writes nothing and ends by `SIGABRT`, and `--interp` reports a run-time error naming the trap (`arithmetic overflow (checked +)`). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §9.2 described a message `concrete: arithmetic overflow in function 'f' at file:line` with the operation, the operands and the policy. Options: (a) the binary writes nothing; (b) it writes one line naming the trap and its location; (c) the §9.2 form. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-101 | see OPEN-101 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): One line naming the trap and location. Its form, decided 2026-11-06: [runtime row](#decided-project-runtime-2026-11-06) |
# Concrete Philosophy

Status: standing policy, the one home of the project's design rules. Every other document links
here instead of restating them. The language rules themselves are the
[specification](../legacy/docs/spec/README.md) and the owner's [decision record](decisions.md); where this
document cites one, those are authoritative, and nothing here overrides a decided entry.

It absorbs, as of 2026-11-04, the principles and design policy of [PROJECT.md](../legacy/docs/project/PROJECT.md), the
refusal list of `ANTI_FEATURES.md` and the "no" and "not yet" entries of PROJECT.md's language
decisions. The per-language comparisons that used to sit beside each refusal are in
[INFLUENCES.md](../legacy/docs/project/INFLUENCES.md#how-other-languages-answer-concretes-refusals).

Contents:

1. [What Concrete is for](#what-concrete-is-for)
2. [The rules](#the-rules)
3. [When rules conflict](#when-rules-conflict)
4. [Admission test](#admission-test)
5. [What Concrete refuses](#what-concrete-refuses)
6. [Where the earlier rules went](#where-the-earlier-rules-went)

## What Concrete is for

**Concrete is a fully proven systems programming language with no garbage collector, where you can
know everything a program does: what it does, what it may touch, whether it can fail or stop, and
what is proved about it.** "Fully proven" means compiler correctness proved end to end, with every
unproven step named as an explicit assumption and never silently trusted, and a program's own
correctness proved by Lean theorems about it. This states the project's goal: each guarantee holds
only as its evidence lands, and native claims remain conditional on their named assumptions
([decision](decisions.md#decided-project-core-statement)).

Concrete is a small systems language for code that must be reviewed, audited, bounded and proved:
no garbage collector and no hidden runtime, linear ownership, second-class references, and
authority that is visible in every signature. Its aim is that a reader can answer "what does this
code do, what may it touch, and what is actually proved about it" from the source, the compiler's
reports and the Lean theorems, without trusting anything that is not named. Lean 4 is the language
the semantics and proofs are written in and the language the compiler is built in; Concrete itself
stays a systems language, not a proof assistant.

Most Concrete code is expected to be written by language models, not typed by hand. What matters
is that everything is checkable and easy to understand: the cost that counts is the cost for a
reviewer, an auditor or a checker to understand what code does and what it may touch. Rule 4
draws the consequence for verbosity.

## The rules

Each rule states what holds and why. Its history is in the changelog and the decision record.

1. **Pick the best long-term structural solution.** A design question is answered by the structure
   that removes the whole class of problem, not by the smallest local patch, and once that answer is
   known it is chosen rather than offered as one option among several. *Why:* a patch fixes one
   instance and leaves the class to recur; the language is still small enough to change shape.

2. **One form per situation.** There are never two ways to write one thing. A second spelling, an
   alias or a convenience form beside an existing one is removed, not added. *Why:* every extra form
   is one more thing a reader, a checker and a proof must cover, and two forms drift apart. The
   decided removals are listed in [OD61](decisions.md#od61) and the entries it names.

3. **The most transparent long-term option wins.** Between two designs, prefer the one that leaves
   nothing for the reader to reconstruct: no hidden control flow, data flow, allocation, dispatch,
   authority or cleanup. *Why:* Concrete's value is that behaviour can be read off the source.

4. **Verbosity never argues against a clearer or safer design.** Writing more is acceptable when it
   makes behaviour, authority or failure visible. "It is longer" is not a reason to reject a
   design; "it hides something" is. *Why:* most code is now written by tools, where writing explicit
import ConcreteSpec.Spec.Basics

/-!
# Part 5: allocator brands

PROPOSED: not normative until the owner freezes Core (ROADMAP A2c). Part of `ConcreteSpec.Spec`;
see `ConcreteSpec/README.md`.
-/

namespace ConcreteSpec.Brand
open Core (AList)

/-! ## Part 5. Allocator brands (Q1.1–Q1.5, Q1.13): `reset_fresh`

An allocator's brand is an erased kind (Q1.1) with no runtime representation. Each `new` and
`reset` binder introduces a brand that differs from every other in scope (generative, Q1.2), and
brands are equal only when they have the same name (nominal, Q1.4). A heap value records the
brand of the allocator it came from, and operations on it require that allocator's brand.
Resources are consumed before their allocator (Q1.5): releasing or resetting an allocator
requires that no value of its brand is live. A `while` loop (Q9.3, Core's one loop) that carries
an allocator carries `∃ β. alloc β` (Q1.3): each iteration, and the code after the loop, opens it
with a fresh name. The loop's condition region is abstracted: the ghost run may stop or iterate
at each test, so the results hold for every outcome of the conditions. Capability checking of the
allocator operations (Q5, Q2.7) is not modelled here (part 6 models capabilities in its own
fragment).

Three views of the same programs: the static check (`Ok`); a ghost run (`Run`, executable as
`ghost`) that gives every allocator a run-time brand from a counter and checks brand equality on
each operation; and the erased run (`erased`), the program as it runs with no brands (Q1.1). A
checked program passes every ghost check (`checked_passes_brand_checks`), and its erased run does
exactly what its ghost run does, brands removed (`brand_erasure`), for every outcome of its loop
tests and any fuel.
-/

/-! ### The static check -/

/-- Static types: an allocator of brand `β`, or a heap value allocated from brand `β`. Both are
linear. -/
inductive STy where
  | alloc (β : Nat)
  | heap (β : Nat)
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

abbrev Ctx := AList Nat STy

/-- No value of brand `β` is live. -/
def NoHeap (Γ : Ctx) (β : Nat) : Prop := ∀ e ∈ Γ, e.2 ≠ .heap β

instance (Γ : Ctx) (β : Nat) : Decidable (NoHeap Γ β) := by unfold NoHeap; infer_instance

inductive Stmt where
  /-- `new a` with brand binder `β`. -/
  | newAlloc (a β : Nat)
  /-- Allocate `h` from `a`. -/
  | alloc (h a : Nat)
  /-- An operation on `h` through `a`. -/
  | use (h a : Nat)
  /-- Free `h` through `a`. -/
  | free (h a : Nat)
  /-- Release `a`. -/
  | release (a : Nat)
  /-- `a' = reset(a)`, with brand binder `β'` for `a'`. -/
  | reset (a' a β' : Nat)
  /-- `while cond { body }` carrying allocator `a`; `β` names its brand inside one iteration and
  `βx` after the loop. The condition is abstracted. -/
  | while_ (a β βx : Nat) (body : List Stmt)

/-- The static check, from the brand names in use `B` and the context `Γ` to the context at the
end. A brand binder must be fresh in `B` (generative); brand equality is name equality
(nominal). -/
inductive Ok : List Nat → Ctx → List Stmt → Ctx → Prop where
  | nil {B Γ} : Ok B Γ [] Γ
  | newAlloc {B Γ a β rest Γ'} : AList.lookup Γ a = none → β ∉ B →
      Ok (β :: B) ((a, .alloc β) :: Γ) rest Γ' → Ok B Γ (.newAlloc a β :: rest) Γ'
  | alloc {B Γ h a β rest Γ'} : AList.lookup Γ a = some (.alloc β) → AList.lookup Γ h = none →
      Ok B ((h, .heap β) :: Γ) rest Γ' → Ok B Γ (.alloc h a :: rest) Γ'
  | use {B Γ h a β rest Γ'} : AList.lookup Γ h = some (.heap β) →
      AList.lookup Γ a = some (.alloc β) →
      Ok B Γ rest Γ' → Ok B Γ (.use h a :: rest) Γ'
  | free {B Γ h a β rest Γ'} : AList.lookup Γ h = some (.heap β) →
      AList.lookup Γ a = some (.alloc β) →
      Ok B (AList.erase Γ h) rest Γ' → Ok B Γ (.free h a :: rest) Γ'
  | release {B Γ a β rest Γ'} : AList.lookup Γ a = some (.alloc β) → NoHeap Γ β →
      Ok B (AList.erase Γ a) rest Γ' → Ok B Γ (.release a :: rest) Γ'
  | reset {B Γ a' a β β' rest Γ'} : AList.lookup Γ a = some (.alloc β) → NoHeap Γ β →
      if 2 * r > den then q + 1 else if 2 * r < den then q else if q % 2 == 1 then q + 1 else q
    let readsBack (n : Nat) (d : Int) : Bool :=
      let v := if d ≤ 0 then floatOfDecimalMantissa (n * 10 ^ (-d).toNat) 0
               else floatOfDecimalMantissa n d.toNat
      n != 0 && v.toBits == pos.toBits
    -- Start at the coarsest precision that could hold the integer part (`d = -(digits)`,
    -- whose `N` is 0 or 1) and refine one digit at a time; the first `d` with a candidate
    -- that reads back is the shortest. At each `d` the nearest candidate `N` is tried
    -- first, then `N - 1` and `N + 1`: at a power of two the rounding interval is
    -- asymmetric, so the nearest `d`-digit decimal can fall outside it while a neighbour
    -- falls inside (2^-24 is `0.00000005960464477539063`, whose nearest 23-digit
    -- neighbour does not read back). Exactness is reached by `d = 1074`, so the fuel never
    -- runs out.
    let intDigits : Nat := (toString (scaled 0)).length
    let rec go (d : Int) (fuel : Nat) : String :=
      let n := scaled d
      match fuel with
      | 0 => render n d
      | fuel + 1 =>
        if readsBack n d then render n d
        else if n > 0 && readsBack (n - 1) d then render (n - 1) d
        else if readsBack (n + 1) d then render (n + 1) d
        else go (d + 1) fuel
    if sig == 0 then sgn ++ "0.0" else go (-(intDigits : Int)) (intDigits + 1100)

/-- Lex a number literal (integer or float), including hex/bin/oct. -/
partial def lexNumberLoop (s : LexerState) (acc : Nat) : LexerState × TokenKind :=
  -- If acc is 0, check for hex/bin/oct prefix
  if acc == 0 then
    -- Bug 220: a radix prefix needs at least one digit of its radix (grammar: `0x hex`); `0x`,
    -- `0b` and `0o` alone used to lex as the literal 0.
    let digitAfter (ok : Char → Bool) : Bool := match s.peekAt 1 with
      | some c => ok c
      | none => false
    let isHex := fun (c : Char) => c.isDigit || (c >= 'a' && c <= 'f') || (c >= 'A' && c <= 'F')
    match s.peek with
    | some 'x' | some 'X' =>
      if digitAfter isHex then lexHexLoop s.advance 0
      else (s.advance, .lexError "a `0x` literal needs at least one hexadecimal digit")
    | some 'b' | some 'B' =>
      if digitAfter (fun c => c == '0' || c == '1') then lexBinLoop s.advance 0
      else (s.advance, .lexError "a `0b` literal needs at least one binary digit")
    | some 'o' | some 'O' =>
      if digitAfter (fun c => c >= '0' && c <= '7') then lexOctLoop s.advance 0
      else (s.advance, .lexError "a `0o` literal needs at least one octal digit")
    | _ => lexDecLoop s acc

